  "structure": {
    "block_count": 51,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "Love Seafood? A Smart Guide to Eating Fish When You're Trying to Get Pregnant",
        "text_sha256": "bf0df4077c46415ff98fd7b517b5d4c1cfb31233a2dd38a83d96d65540fd983a",
        "word_count": 14
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Updates to copy, sources, and formatting.",
        "text_sha256": "2bf448333081ffc8549a45a6c4b7fc711ee4b93575f0fffd3c4a6212c13581c2",
        "word_count": 6
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "plate of fish",
        "text_sha256": "bc93eb16f24621c2051a3cf837d1b3e62d9610a3a169bfe43b9068d996cc59c8",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Fish is packed with nutrients that support a healthy pre-pregnancy diet, but mercury levels mean some choices are better than others.",
        "text_sha256": "24c1af9f0993c73f72562e45f6f10475b414dc88a97839f0903bdb8f502787ac",
        "word_count": 21
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Seafood and fish are filled with protein, vitamins, and heart-healthy omega-3 fatty acids — essential nutrients when you're on a pre-pregnancy diet.",
        "text_sha256": "a02a7b50328844901384b3c4f0a1a5bb610b4ad408cfb45500ee78b20c681c0f",
        "word_count": 22
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "But when you're trying to conceive, it's important to keep a keen eye on the fish you eat, because some contain high levels of the heavy metal mercury, a toxin that can damage the nervous system. Here's what you need to know about eating fish safely before you get pregnant.",
        "text_sha256": "44f8c195087813f3ed7bb875f8417a70e8cf79bc14be106eca4f31e488d26027",
        "word_count": 50
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Key Takeaways",
        "text_sha256": "cc3f7f0f4513969e418b56fb93ec0876fd96dd6498395b04301f70dbddff8a45",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Fish is not only safe when you’re trying to conceive — it’s encouraged. The FDA and EPA recommend 8 to 12 ounces of low-mercury fish per week to fuel your body with protein and brain-boosting omega-3s.",
        "text_sha256": "54ab74b6b09cbb4c30b2e77fb49c0612a447b580a54ffec044ea0387ec284fb3",
        "word_count": 36
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Most fish are fair game, but mercury matters. Stick with low-mercury options like salmon, shrimp, and canned light tuna, and steer clear of high-mercury fish such as swordfish, shark, and king mackerel.",
        "text_sha256": "7ede9cf2da42f54fcf5d9e54539bae315587ecfaa664f74224f43fe446ad1861",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Not a seafood fan? You still have options. Prenatal vitamins, fish oil supplements, algae-based DHA, or even DHA-fortified eggs can help you get the omega-3s your body needs before pregnancy.",
        "text_sha256": "235b37b9e5061f5899e958ab0fd43e6e0eecdced4d8ac3c41abef2ff4c5703e6",
        "word_count": 30
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Is it safe to eat fish when you're trying to conceive?",
        "text_sha256": "0eec470be35e066a90df1a14508b91711ea6f1f0f2602d52290a3db79f055eb7",
        "word_count": 11
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "In short: absolutely. Fish is considered one of the best foods for women who are trying to get pregnant.",
        "text_sha256": "15b87ac8e38f3c8e89313c99cb88ac605f3c6a99cea7235096b303382f8b3871",
        "word_count": 19
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "In fact, the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) and the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) jointly recommend that women who are pregnant or working on getting pregnant eat 8 to 12 ounces of low-mercury fish per week because it contains lots of healthy protein, plus brain-boosting DHA. Trusted SourceEnvironmental Protection AgencyEPA-FDA Advice about Eating Fish and ShellfishSee All Sources [1]",
        "text_sha256": "576e98ca9826d9a011ec2badc045730398b34787c5c117a7a98162a1a50d9707",
        "word_count": 59
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceEnvironmental Protection AgencyEPA-FDA Advice about Eating Fish and ShellfishSee All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "2265c78fcafa34656adcea3d6cae91fbc90c3934526fc29460e67cf28f4349cc",
        "word_count": 12
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "That's two to three 4-ounce servings of, say, canned light tuna, or one and a half servings of a typical fish steak or filet — in other words, about two to three meals per week.",
        "text_sha256": "9920459c73ef6a1b994600115b03e6f19bc7236c2b4ebcda7f421a4d3fbeecd5",
        "word_count": 35
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "What are the best types of fish to eat if you're trying to get pregnant?",
        "text_sha256": "d62bd67561ee013633739141b3bcc4533d96422f8ab24021fbf0f7c37254bbd5",
        "word_count": 15
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
        "block_index": 16,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "As beneficial as seafood is for future moms-to-be, it's important to know your fish. The FDA and EPA say that 90% of the fish you'll buy in the U.S. is considered a great choice to eat at two to three servings per week. Great choices include (but are not limited to): Trusted SourceFood & Drug AdministrationAdvice About Eating Fish for Those Who Might Become or Are Pregnant or Breastfeeding and Children Ages 1 - 11 YearsSee All Sources [2]",
        "text_sha256": "0692543cf244e9e349fd631778b5d257e5653c8ebd6f2cfc11e4997f16ed50d2",
        "word_count": 79
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000017",
        "block_index": 17,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceFood & Drug AdministrationAdvice About Eating Fish for Those Who Might Become or Are Pregnant or Breastfeeding and Children Ages 1 - 11 YearsSee All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "0455c52c2174fd485857d78d6fd2062c11e325e81ae1a2157708d6b0af31565f",
        "word_count": 27
      },
      {
